# f05: the p2Char-load-bearing VS path -> g03 params (falcon / fox /
# pstadium; iter 90, review-88 M2). Full-stream bridge vs the frozen g03
# oracle stream, so p2Char != 0 is stream-load-bearing here.
# NOTE (registered brief amendment, AGENT-LOG iter 90): the triage's
# literal target (CPU d9 -> m02) is unreachable through the faithful
# UI — the upstream CSS slider domain is 1..4 (css.js:326-327), so d9
# cannot be expressed in upstream's own CSS either; g03 carries the
# finding's core (p2Char != 0) with a frozen full-stream oracle.
#
# RE-AUTHORED for the CSS mechanics arc (docs/MENU-SPEC.md items 1+2+3+4).
# WHY THE SHAPE CHANGED SO MUCH: g03 is human-vs-human, and upstream's
# ownership guard (css.js:300, `playerType[j] == 1 || i == j`) says a hand
# may take its OWN token or any CPU port's — never another human's. This
# device has ONE hand (foh.h PORT MODEL), so port 2's token is reachable
# only while port 2 is CPU. The leg therefore does exactly that: put port
# 2 on CPU, take its token, hover fox, drop, then walk the type box back
# round to HMN (CPU -> N/A -> HMN, DEVIATION D5's 3-cycle). Every step is
# an upstream-legal gesture; togglePort never touches chosenChar
# (main.js:504-520), so the pick survives the walk-back.
#
# CLAMP-ANCHORED POSITIONING — see the note in f01-vs-g01.flow.
